      "body": [
        {
          "type": "TextualBody",
          "value": "\n<ol><li>\n<p>four hours, [and] 438 parts, so that when Nisan is born at four hours of the</p>\n<p>day, Tishri</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>will be born at eight hours 438 parts; and if we postpone Nisan because of</p>\n<p>Tishri, this contradicts his rule</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>that Nisan before noon is not postponed; and if we do not postpone Tishri</p>\n<p>because of Nisan,</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>this contradicts his rule that Tishri after noon is postponed. And if {will [nb: scribe begins misplaced text here] be a full</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>hour plus 256 parts, and so 589 will be a full hour [and] 150 parts, and so all</p>\n<p>of them</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>by adding 641. And the intelligent will understand (Dan. 12:10). As to the</p>\n<p>second [argument], in which he said that the calculation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>of the 4 Gates only covers 4 [week]days alone and a minority of hours and</p>\n<p>parts,</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>his discourse demonstrates that he has not gone past, as it were, the</p>\n<p>entrance of this art. For the calculation of the 4</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Gates includes all the [week]days, and among them some [week]days are</p>\n<p>mentioned two or three</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>times&mdash;but whoever is wise, let him understand these (Hosea 14:10). And</p>\n<p>behold, I shall explain this briefly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>[\"If any Tishri in whose year there is an inter]calation is born before 491</p>\n<p>[parts] of the</p>\n</li></ol>\n",
          "format": "text/html"
        }
